chapters.push({id:'design',week:'8–10',title:'Designing and testing solutions',intro:'Turn a problem into a working solution you can justify. Develop a club-order manager through investigation, design, development and evaluation.',objectives:['Write measurable requirements and a realistic schedule.','Design modular algorithms using a structure chart and stubs.','Select tests, debug systematically and evaluate evidence against requirements.'],sections:[
['Investigate: understand the problem',"<p>A school club sells badges for $3 each. The organiser needs a console program to record orders, calculate costs, save records and summarise the session. Start by asking what each order contains, which inputs are allowed and what the organiser needs to see.</p><p>For this project, use invented order IDs and quantities. Do not collect real student names or payment details. The table lists what the program must do and a way to check each requirement.</p><table><tr><th>ID</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Acceptance evidence</th></tr><tr><td>R1</td><td>Accept a non-negative whole-number quantity; reject unsuitable input and allow retry.</td><td>Try letters, a decimal string, a negative integer and zero.</td></tr><tr><td>R2</td><td>Calculate badge cost at 300 cents per badge.</td><td>Quantities 0, 1 and 4 give 0, 300 and 1200 cents.</td></tr><tr><td>R3</td><td>Store the quantity of each accepted order in a session list.</td><td>Enter 2, 0 and 5; confirm three elements in the correct order.</td></tr><tr><td>R4</td><td>Append each accepted order as ID, quantity and cost to a text file.</td><td>Check the file after two runs; previous records must remain.</td></tr><tr><td>R5</td><td>Display the session's total quantity, total cost and first largest-order index, or a clear empty-session message.</td><td>Check typical, tied-maximum and empty sessions.</td></tr></table><p>This brief allows a quantity of zero. Order IDs must be non-empty invented labels with no commas or line breaks, so the file format stays clear. Also state what you rely on, such as having a project folder that the program can write to. These are your <strong>assumptions</strong>.</p>"],
['Plan the work and dependencies',"<p>List the tasks before you start coding. Put them in an order that makes sense: decide the record format before writing file code, and implement a calculation before testing it. A task that must happen first is a <strong>dependency</strong>. Leave time to fix mistakes. A <strong>Gantt chart</strong> places tasks on a timeline; the shaded cells below show when you plan to work on them.</p><table><tr><th>Task</th><th>Week 8</th><th>Week 9</th><th>Week 10</th></tr><tr><td>Requirements, schedule and design</td><td>■</td><td></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Functions and file integration</td><td></td><td>■</td><td>■</td></tr><tr><td>Testing and corrections</td><td>■ Design tests</td><td>■ Unit tests</td><td>■ Whole-system tests</td></tr><tr><td>User trial and evaluation</td><td></td><td></td><td>■</td></tr></table><p>You have ten project lessons: four in week 8, two in week 9 and four in week 10. The other two lessons teach APIs, debugging and attribution. Record what you actually finish beside the plan. If your plan changes, explain why.</p>"],
['Design: divide the solution into modules',"<p>Break the program into smaller tasks, such as validating input, calculating a cost and saving an order. A <strong>structure chart</strong> shows which modules call which other modules. It does not show every decision and loop as a flowchart would.</p><pre>main\n├── read_order_id → order_id\n├── read_quantity → quantity\n├── order_cost(quantity) → cost_cents\n├── save_order(order_id, quantity, cost_cents)\n└── summarise_orders(quantities) → summary</pre><p>Give each module one clear job. Pass in the values it needs and return a result where useful. The main part of the program, called the <strong>mainline</strong>, coordinates these jobs. You can write a function’s name and parameters before its code is ready. This placeholder is a <strong>stub</strong>.</p><pre>def save_order(order_id, quantity, cost_cents):\n    # Stub: deliberately unfinished.\n    raise NotImplementedError(\"Implement file append here\")</pre><p>The stub deliberately raises an error if called. Replace it with working code and test it before using it in the full program.</p><pre>quantities = empty array\nWHILE organiser chooses to add an order\n    read and validate order ID and quantity\n    cost = order_cost(quantity)\n    append record to file\n    append quantity to quantities\nEND WHILE\nIF quantities is empty THEN\n    output empty-session message\nELSE\n    output totals and first maximum position\nEND IF</pre><p>Save the order to the file before adding its quantity to the session list. If saving fails, the program should not report the order as saved. Decide how to explain the failure and allow a retry. Walk through the design with test data before coding.</p>"],
['Develop: build a small working part',"<p>Start with a function that only calculates cost. It should not ask for input or write a file. You can then test it by supplying known numbers. Use integer cents for this fixed-price example and convert to dollars when displaying the result.</p><p>Next add input checking, file storage and the session summary, testing after each change. Use meaningful names and consistent indentation. Explain important choices in comments. Keep dated versions or use an approved version-control tool, and store a backup separately.</p><p>When one part of software asks another part to do a job, it uses an <strong>application programming interface (API)</strong>. For example, Python’s file interface lets your program open and write files. The <code>csv</code> module provides another documented interface. A web API is only one kind: this project does not need a live web service. Check each interface’s inputs, outputs and errors, and acknowledge code you reuse.</p>"],
['Test: choose evidence that can expose mistakes',"<p>Test at three levels:</p><ul><li><strong>Unit test:</strong> check one module by itself, such as the cost calculation.</li><li><strong>Integration test:</strong> check that modules work together, such as passing an accepted order to file storage.</li><li><strong>User acceptance test:</strong> check that the organiser can do what the agreed requirements promise.</li></ul><p>Work out the expected result before running each test.</p><table><tr><th>Test</th><th>Input / action</th><th>Expected result</th><th>Requirement</th></tr><tr><td>T1</td><td>Quantity -1</td><td>Reject; retry allowed</td><td>R1</td></tr><tr><td>T2</td><td>Quantity 0</td><td>Accept; cost 0 cents</td><td>R1–R2</td></tr><tr><td>T3</td><td>Quantity 1</td><td>Cost 300 cents</td><td>R2</td></tr><tr><td>T4</td><td>Text \"three\" or \"2.5\"</td><td>Reject without an unhandled conversion error</td><td>R1</td></tr><tr><td>T5</td><td>Orders 2, 0, 5</td><td>Quantity total 7; cost 2100 cents; largest index 2</td><td>R3, R5</td></tr><tr><td>T6</td><td>Orders 5, 5</td><td>First largest index 0</td><td>R5</td></tr><tr><td>T7</td><td>No orders</td><td>Clear message; no access to index 0</td><td>R5</td></tr><tr><td>T8</td><td>Save one order, restart and save another</td><td>Both records retained with separate lines</td><td>R4</td></tr></table><p>The lowest allowed quantity is zero. Test -1, 0 and 1 to check just below, at and above that boundary. Also test both decision branches, zero/one/several orders, tied largest orders, unsuitable IDs and a file-write failure. Record the actual result, any correction and the retest. Expected results alone do not show that you ran the tests.</p>"],
['Debug: isolate the first incorrect state',"<p>First identify the kind of failure. A missing colon can cause a <strong>syntax error</strong>. An invalid index can cause a <strong>runtime error</strong>. Using write mode instead of append mode can lose records without crashing: that is a <strong>logic error</strong>.</p><p>Find a small example that repeats the failure. Then:</p><ol><li>Predict the values after each step.</li><li>Trace by hand, step through the program or temporarily print the values.</li><li>Find the first value that differs from your prediction.</li><li>Fix the cause, rerun the failed test and rerun earlier tests.</li></ol><p>Rerunning earlier tests checks for <strong>regressions</strong>: problems caused by a change. For a tied largest order, inspect whether the comparison uses <code>&gt;</code> or <code>&gt;=</code>.</p><p>For maximum-position logic, keep a trace table with current index, current quantity and remembered best index. If tied values unexpectedly choose the last order, inspect whether your comparison uses greater-than-or-equal instead of strict greater-than.</p>"],
['Evaluate: judge the result against the brief',"<p>Ask a classmate to act as the organiser. Have them create an order, recover from unsuitable input, check the saved record and request a summary. Watch what happens without guiding every action. For each requirement, use a test or observation to decide whether it is met, partly met or not met.</p><p>Explain a limit and a useful next improvement. “It works well” says little. “R4 passed T8, but saving to a read-only folder still causes an unhandled error” gives evidence and identifies a problem. Reflect on how you worked too: what helped, what delayed you and what you would change next time. This reflection is a <strong>developer retrospective</strong>.</p><p>Keep a source log for borrowed code, media and documentation. Giving credit does not replace licence permission. Describe your own contribution and any assistance you used.</p>"],
['Project pack and checkpoints',"<p><a class=\"button\" href=\"project-starter.py\" download>Download unfinished project starter</a> <a class=\"button secondary\" href=\"project-workbook.md\" download>Download project workbook</a></p><p>The starter has functions for you to complete; it is not a finished solution. Use the workbook to record requirements, your schedule, design choices, tests and evaluation. At a checkpoint, explain how you reached your decisions as well as showing the code.</p><table><tr><th>Week</th><th>Checkpoint</th><th>Bring evidence</th></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Design review</td><td>Requirements, schedule, structure chart, pseudocode and predicted tests</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Build review</td><td>Working modules, unit-test results and a record of corrections</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Demonstration and evaluation</td><td>Program, synthetic data file, completed tests, acceptance evidence and retrospective</td></tr></table><p>Use these checkpoints to discuss your progress and choose what to improve next.</p>"]
],code:'BADGE_PRICE_CENTS = 300\n\ndef order_cost(quantity):\n    if not isinstance(quantity, int) or isinstance(quantity, bool):\n        raise ValueError("Quantity must be a whole number")\n    if quantity < 0:\n        raise ValueError("Quantity cannot be negative")\n    return quantity * BADGE_PRICE_CENTS\n\ndef format_money(cents):\n    return f"${cents // 100}.{cents % 100:02d}"\n\nif __name__ == "__main__":\n    for quantity in [0, 1, 4]:\n        actual = order_cost(quantity)\n        expected = quantity * 300\n        print(quantity, actual, expected, format_money(actual))\n',practice:[['Improve','Rewrite the requirement “the program should be easy to use” so you can test it.','One possible criterion: after an invalid quantity, display a specific message and let the organiser retry without restarting. Observe this with the input three followed by 2.'],['Design','Why put calculation in a function separate from input and file storage?','You can supply known inputs and check returned values without typing or changing files. A failed calculation test can be isolated from input and storage problems.'],['Test','Why are -1, 0 and 1 useful quantity tests?','They are below, at and above the lower valid boundary. A check that accidentally rejects zero will be exposed.'],['Evaluate','All calculation tests pass, but a second run replaces the first record. Is the solution complete?','No. R4 fails. Investigate the file mode, switch to append if appropriate, then rerun the persistence and earlier tests.']],task:"Build the club-order manager from your own design. Keep your code, invented data file and project workbook together. Show how it handles unsuitable input, saves across two runs, reports an empty session and handles a tied largest order. Keep it to a console application that fits the ten project lessons.",solution:'# Illustrative unit-test fragment, not the complete project solution.\ndef order_cost(quantity):\n    if not isinstance(quantity, int) or isinstance(quantity, bool) or quantity < 0:\n        raise ValueError("Invalid quantity")\n    return quantity * 300\n\nassert order_cost(0) == 0\nassert order_cost(1) == 300\nassert order_cost(4) == 1200\ntry:\n    order_cost(-1)\nexcept ValueError:\n    print("Negative quantity correctly rejected")\nelse:\n    raise AssertionError("Negative quantity was accepted")\nprint("Calculation checks passed")\n',recap:"Decide what the program must do. Plan its parts and tests, build a small working version, then use actual results to find mistakes and judge how well it meets the brief.",lessons:['Week 8, lesson 1: agree requirements and acceptance criteria.','Week 8, lesson 2: plan dependencies and the Gantt chart.','Week 8, lesson 3: design modules, data and pseudocode.','Week 8, lesson 4: desk-check the design and review the checkpoint.','Week 9, lesson 1: learn API contracts and code/source attribution.','Week 9, lesson 2: practice error classification and debugging.','Week 9, lesson 3: implement and test calculation/input modules.','Week 9, lesson 4: implement storage and review build evidence.','Week 10, lesson 1: integrate the session summary.','Week 10, lesson 2: execute tests, correct and retest.','Week 10, lesson 3: conduct the organiser acceptance trial.','Week 10, lesson 4: demonstrate, evaluate and write the retrospective.'],reference:'https://docs.python.org/3/library/unittest.html',refTitle:'Python unit testing reference (optional extension)',quiz:{q:'Which is evidence that a solution meets a requirement?',options:['A prediction of the output alone','A recorded actual result compared with a pre-defined expected result','A large amount of code'],answer:1,explanation:'A completed comparison provides test evidence. A prediction is only the test plan.'}});
